["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Grouse are creatures of habit with a few predictable tricks. Read the bird and you know when it will go, where it will land, and why the old ones are hard.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two grouse. Relaxed: head low, feeding, feathers loose. About to go: neck stretched tall, crest raised, tail fanning. Tip, hunter lore and not a study: when you see the tall neck, stop, shoulder the gun and take one more step.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Read the bird (Tip)</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"44\" width=\"185\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"205\" y=\"44\" width=\"185\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fbe3d3\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<g transform=\"translate(110 150) scale(1.4 1.4)\"><polygon points=\"-20,-6 -38,-20 -48,-12 -52,0 -48,8 -20,8\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><path d=\"M-40 -18 Q-50 -10 -48 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"15\" fill=\"#9a6b45\"/><circle cx=\"21\" cy=\"-14\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#9a6b45\"/><polygon points=\"28,-16 35,-13 28,-10\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><circle cx=\"23\" cy=\"-16\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#23261f\"/><path d=\"M2 13 l-2 11 M10 13 l2 11\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\"/></g>\n<g transform=\"translate(300 160)\"><polygon points=\"-30,-8 -60,-34 -66,-10 -64,10 -30,8\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"17\" fill=\"#9a6b45\"/><rect x=\"16\" y=\"-52\" width=\"12\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#9a6b45\"/><circle cx=\"24\" cy=\"-58\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#9a6b45\"/><polygon points=\"18,-66 22,-80 28,-66\" fill=\"#9a6b45\"/><polygon points=\"32,-60 42,-57 32,-53\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><circle cx=\"27\" cy=\"-60\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#23261f\"/><path d=\"M4 16 l-2 14 M14 16 l2 14\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Relaxed</text>\n<text x=\"298\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#c23b22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">About to go</text>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Head low, feeding</text>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Feathers loose</text>\n<text x=\"298\" y=\"204\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Neck stretched tall</text>\n<text x=\"298\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Crest up, tail fans</text>\n<text x=\"10\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">See the tall neck? Stop, shoulder the gun,</text>\n<text x=\"10\" y=\"288\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">step once more. Hunter lore, not a study.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>A tall neck and a raised crest: the bird is about to go (Tip).</li><li>Clucks and a fanned tail often come first (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: alarm signals</summary><ul><li>Alert posture: neck stretched up, crest raised, head turning; the tail may fan (hunter lore, 60%).</li><li>Alarm sounds: grouse cluck or \"putt\" before flushing, say many hunters (hunter lore, 55%: Tip). Exact calls not confirmed (<span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>).</li><li>A hen with chicks hisses, fans her tail and ruff, and may run around you or fake a broken wing to lead you off (70%, Macaulay Library recording notes and field accounts).</li><li>A sooty or blue grouse hen with small chicks clucks, fluffs, droops her wings and may fly at an intruder <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>In September a bird that walks off fast with a raised crest is not staying (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A scale from lets you close to flushes far, hunter lore. Spruce grouse, the fool hen, at the tame end. Dusky and sooty grouse next. Sharp tailed grouse early in the season in the middle. Ruffed grouse toward the wild end. Sharp tailed grouse late in the season at the wild end. Tip: every bird learns, and a bird shot at or chased gets wilder.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"280\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Tame or wild? (Tip, hunter lore)</text>\n<defs><linearGradient id=\"mgr-tame-scale-g\" x1=\"0\" x2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#8a9a6b\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#e8590c\"/></linearGradient></defs>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"120\" width=\"360\" height=\"22\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"url(#mgr-tame-scale-g)\"/>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lets you close</text>\n<text x=\"380\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#c23b22\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Flushes far</text>\n<polygon points=\"40,146 33,160 47,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Spruce</text>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">&quot;fool hen&quot;</text>\n<polygon points=\"120,146 113,160 127,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"120\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dusky,</text>\n<text x=\"120\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">sooty</text>\n<polygon points=\"215,146 208,160 222,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sharp tail,</text>\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">early</text>\n<polygon points=\"290,146 283,160 297,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"290\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Ruffed</text>\n<text x=\"290\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text>\n<polygon points=\"355,146 348,160 362,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"355\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sharp tail,</text>\n<text x=\"355\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">late</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Every bird learns. A bird shot at or chased</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">by a dog gets wilder (Tip).</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Spruce grouse often sit; ruffed grouse go (75 to 80%).</li><li>Every grouse gets warier after it is shot at (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what makes the difference</summary><ul><li>Spruce grouse rely on stillness and camouflage and often let people approach closely: the \"fool hen\" (80%, Idaho Fish and Game).</li><li>Dusky and sooty grouse are often approachable, especially young birds early in the season (Tip, 60%).</li><li>Sharp tailed grouse: singles and small groups early in the season are easier; late season flocks flush far and seldom give a second chance (60%, hunting guides).</li><li>Ruffed grouse: the wary one in most hunters' books (Tip).</li><li>Habitat counts too: open cover means longer flushes; studies on a European grouse found less visual cover meant longer flush distances <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 310\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bar chart from a Pennsylvania Game Commission study, 75 percent. Share of grouse shot each year: 48 percent near open roads, 19 percent farther away. Males alive after a year: 23 percent within 201 metres of a road, 36 percent beyond 201 metres.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"310\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Roads get birds shot (Pennsylvania study)</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Share of birds shot each year</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Near open roads</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"92\" width=\"240.0\" height=\"24\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"266.0\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">48%</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"134\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Farther away</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"142\" width=\"95.0\" height=\"24\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"121.0\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">19%</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Males alive after a year</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Within 201 m of a road</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"228\" width=\"115.00000000000001\" height=\"24\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"141.0\" y=\"246\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">23%</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Beyond 201 m</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"278\" width=\"180.0\" height=\"24\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"206.0\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">36%</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Birds near open roads get shot more <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Birds farther from roads live longer <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the evidence</summary><ul><li>Pennsylvania study: harvest rate was 48% near open roads versus 19% farther away <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Male survival was 36% beyond 201 m from roads versus 23% within 201 m <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>A North Carolina study saw grouse change their movements to avoid areas after flushing and shooting <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Hunting pressure did not lower populations in that study's small woodlots from fall to fall <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Hinterland Who's Who: most hunted birds are young birds near roads and clearings <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Old hand's rule (tradition): \"The easy birds live by the road. The good birds live past the first ridge.\"</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 290\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline of a ruffed grouse family. June: hatch. Week 1: short hops. Week 3: flies well. Week 12: the family splits. September and October: the young move away. In a Wisconsin study, young hens moved about 4.8 kilometres and young males about 2.1 kilometres over 2 to 3 weeks, 80 percent. Adults stay home on a few hectares.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"290\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A ruffed grouse family year</text>\n<line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"120\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">June</text>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hatch</text>\n<circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"120\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/>\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Week 1</text>\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Short hops</text>\n<circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"120\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/>\n<text x=\"170\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Week 3</text>\n<text x=\"170\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Flies well</text>\n<circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"120\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/>\n<text x=\"250\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Week 12</text>\n<text x=\"250\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Family splits</text>\n<circle cx=\"340\" cy=\"120\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/>\n<text x=\"340\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sept, Oct</text>\n<text x=\"340\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Young move</text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"200\" width=\"380\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"22\" y=\"224\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Young hens move about 4.8 km, young males</text>\n<text x=\"22\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">about 2.1 km, over 2 to 3 weeks (80%).</text>\n<text x=\"22\" y=\"260\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Adults stay home on a few hectares.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Ruffed: hen raises the brood alone; males live alone <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>At about 12 weeks the family breaks up and young birds move out <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: social life, species by species</summary><ul><li>Ruffed grouse males hold a territory for life and drum to claim it (85%, Hinterland Who's Who). Hens range wider and pass through several males' areas <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Families break up when the young are about 12 weeks old <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Young hens moved about 4.8 km, young males about 2.1 km, over 17 to 21 days in Wisconsin (80%, Small and Rusch 1989).</li><li>This \"fall shuffle\" makes young birds restless. Some fly into windows, fences and trees (75%, Pennsylvania Game Commission).</li><li>Dusky grouse: summer groups of about 6 to 7 (a hen and chicks); winter groups of 15 to 20 seen (65%, Animal Diversity Web).</li><li>Sharp tailed grouse: males gather on leks to display; in late fall birds bunch into big flocks (60 to 85%).</li><li>Ptarmigan: in autumn young and adults form small flocks, which can merge (85%, Hinterland Who's Who). Male willow ptarmigan help raise the young, unlike other grouse <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bar chart, Pennsylvania Game Commission, 75 percent. Share of adults among grouse shot: 34 percent in October, 51 percent in November, 57 percent in December. Early in the season most birds shot are naive young birds; late in the season, more are adults.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Adults in the bag, by month (Pennsylvania)</text>\n<rect x=\"60\" y=\"128\" width=\"70\" height=\"102\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/>\n<text x=\"95\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">34%</text>\n<text x=\"95\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Oct</text>\n<rect x=\"170\" y=\"77\" width=\"70\" height=\"153\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/>\n<text x=\"205\" y=\"69\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">51%</text>\n<text x=\"205\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nov</text>\n<rect x=\"280\" y=\"59\" width=\"70\" height=\"171\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/>\n<text x=\"315\" y=\"51\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">57%</text>\n<text x=\"315\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dec</text>\n<line x1=\"40\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"230\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"282\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Early: you shoot naive young birds. Late: adults.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Early season: mostly naive young birds in the bag <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Late season: more adults, who know every escape <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the old male</summary><ul><li>Pennsylvania: adults were 34% of the harvest in October, 51% in November, 57% in December <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Adult males keep the same territory for life and know every escape route (85% territory; escape routes are hunter lore, Tip).</li><li>An old bird often flushes wild, at the edge of range, with a tree between you (hunter lore, 60%).</li><li>Young birds sit longer, fly into the open and land in plain sight (Tip).</li><li>Old hand's rule (tradition): \"September teaches the birds. November tests the hunter.\"</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram anim\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animation. A grouse flushes from a bush, glides in an arc and lands in a conifer about 80 metres away. A circle marks the tree. You wait a moment, then walk straight toward the marked tree, ready for a second flush.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Flush, glide, land: mark it</text>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"230\" width=\"400\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"232\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"18\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<polygon points=\"330,122 280.5,232 379.5,232\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"327\" y=\"232\" width=\"6\" height=\"16\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n<path id=\"mgr-follow-up-p\" d=\"M70 220 Q120 120 200 130 Q280 140 318 172\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 6\"/>\n<g><g transform=\"translate(0 0) scale(0.55 0.55)\"><polygon points=\"-20,-6 -38,-20 -48,-12 -52,0 -48,8 -20,8\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><path d=\"M-40 -18 Q-50 -10 -48 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"15\" fill=\"#9a6b45\"/><circle cx=\"21\" cy=\"-14\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#9a6b45\"/><polygon points=\"16,-20 20,-28 25,-20\" fill=\"#9a6b45\"/><polygon points=\"28,-16 35,-13 28,-10\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><circle cx=\"23\" cy=\"-16\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#23261f\"/><path d=\"M2 13 l-2 11 M10 13 l2 11\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\"/></g><animateMotion dur=\"3s\" fill=\"freeze\"><mpath href=\"#mgr-follow-up-p\"/></animateMotion></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"3s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><circle cx=\"318\" cy=\"172\" r=\"16\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/></g>\n<g><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"262\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"translate\" values=\"0 0;0 0;250 -6\" keyTimes=\"0;0.45;1\" dur=\"7s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></g>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">You</text>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"3.2s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"44\" width=\"240\" height=\"30\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mark the tree. Go now.</text></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"7s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Second flush: be ready</text></g>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>Mark the tree. Go now. <button class=\"anim-replay\">↻ Replay</button></figcaption></figure><ul><li>They glide a short way and land in a tree <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>They return to the same food and grit spots (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: mistakes you can use</summary><ul><li>Short flight: most ruffed grouse fly under 90 m (100 yd) and land in trees (60 to 75%). Mark the spot and walk straight there.</li><li>Walking away under an evergreen, then flushing from the far side: circle the tree (Tip, Outdoor Canada).</li><li>Same spots: grit on the same road edge, dust in the same bowl, drum from the same log again and again (Tip; Hinterland Who's Who notes males hold territories for life, 85%).</li><li>Young birds in September and October sit on road edges eating clover and grit <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Spruce grouse often stay put after the first shot (Tip). Be sure of your species and your bag count before the next.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A scale from lets you close to flushes far, hunter lore. Spruce grouse, the fool hen, at the tame end. Dusky and sooty grouse next. Sharp tailed grouse early in the season in the middle. Ruffed grouse toward the wild end. Sharp tailed grouse late in the season at the wild end. Tip: every bird learns, and a bird shot at or chased gets wilder.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"280\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Tame or wild? (Tip, hunter lore)</text>\n<defs><linearGradient id=\"mgr-tame-scale-g\" x1=\"0\" x2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#8a9a6b\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#e8590c\"/></linearGradient></defs>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"120\" width=\"360\" height=\"22\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"url(#mgr-tame-scale-g)\"/>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lets you close</text>\n<text x=\"380\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#c23b22\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Flushes far</text>\n<polygon points=\"40,146 33,160 47,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Spruce</text>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">&quot;fool hen&quot;</text>\n<polygon points=\"120,146 113,160 127,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"120\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dusky,</text>\n<text x=\"120\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">sooty</text>\n<polygon points=\"215,146 208,160 222,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sharp tail,</text>\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">early</text>\n<polygon points=\"290,146 283,160 297,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"290\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Ruffed</text>\n<text x=\"290\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text>\n<polygon points=\"355,146 348,160 362,160\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n<text x=\"355\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sharp tail,</text>\n<text x=\"355\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">late</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Every bird learns. A bird shot at or chased</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">by a dog gets wilder (Tip).</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Rhyme: \"Neck goes tall, bird goes all.\"</li><li>Number trick: \"12 weeks, then they seek\": families split at about 12 weeks.</li><li>Old hand's rule (tradition): \"Mark the tree, go now.\"</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks</summary><ul><li>\"48 by the road, 19 off it\": harvest rates near and away from roads.</li><li>\"Young birds in October, old birds in December.\"</li><li>\"Fool hen sits, ruffed hen splits.\"</li><li>Rhymes are made for this course; the \"mark and follow\" habit is common advice in grouse hunting writing.</li></ul></details>","<ul><li>Pennsylvania Game Commission, Hinterland Who's Who, Small and Rusch 1989, hunting writers. Checked 2026-10-11.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source</summary><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.pa.gov/agencies/pgc/wildlife/discover-pa-wildlife/ruffed-grouse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pennsylvania Game Commission, Ruffed grouse</a>, read directly <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.hww.ca/en/wildlife/birds/ruffed-grouse.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hinterland Who's Who, Ruffed grouse</a> and <a href=\"https://www.hww.ca/assets/pdfs/factsheets/ptarmigan-en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ptarmigan</a>, read directly <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://sora.unm.edu/sites/default/files/journals/auk/v106n01/p0072-p0079.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Small and Rusch 1989, The natal dispersal of ruffed grouse, The Auk 106</a>, abstract via search <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://seafwa.org/journal/1973/effects-hunting-ruffed-grouse-populations-small-woodlots-ashe-and-alleghany-counties\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Effects of hunting on ruffed grouse in small woodlots, North Carolina</a>, search preview <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://idfg.idaho.gov/press/field-forest-grouse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Idaho Fish and Game, Field and forest grouse</a>, read directly <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://animaldiversity.org/accounts/Dendragapus_obscurus/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Animal Diversity Web, dusky grouse</a>, read directly <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Macaulay Library recording 2381 notes, ruffed grouse hen with chicks, search preview <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Capercaillie flush distance study, Journal of Wildlife Management 71, search preview <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Sharp tailed grouse late season flocks: MeatEater and Project Upland guides, search preview <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.outdoorlife.com/articles/hunting/2007/09/hit-or-miss-grouse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Outdoor Life, Hit or miss grouse</a> and <a href=\"https://www.outdoorcanada.ca/?p=68\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Outdoor Canada</a>, search preview <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>All checked 2026-10-11. Full log: <code>research/2026-10-11-masterclass-grouse.md</code>.</li></ul></details>"]